From: Danigamer05 <96824289+Danigamer05@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 05:23:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 09/38] A loop limit for compute programs alone (hair strands
 no longer scatter with VICTORY_SHADER_LOOP_LIMIT=32)

A lane that reaches the translated loop limit leaves the program without
its writes. The low limit NVIDIA Turing cards run with
(VICTORY_SHADER_LOOP_LIMIT=32; fxceless/victory-fork#5 loses the device
without it) also cuts genuine compute loops short: the hair simulation's
per-vertex constraint pass (smh_sim_*:CS_SolvePerVertConstraint) loops
once per vertex of a strand, more than 32 times, so its lanes never
stored and the strands drawn from the simulation buffers scattered
around the head, different every frame.

VICTORY_SHADER_LOOP_LIMIT_CS=<n> (diagnostics builds) sets the limit for
compute programs only; unset, they follow VICTORY_SHADER_LOOP_LIMIT as
before. The shader cache keys on it.

Measured on an RTX 2080 SUPER (prologue world, 25-30 min runs, no device
loss in any):
- c96b4f2 + this, VICTORY_SHADER_LOOP_LIMIT=32 only: strands scattered
  (unchanged), 0.98 fps.
- + VICTORY_SHADER_LOOP_LIMIT_CS=128: hair in place in every sampled world
  frame (a single stray strand in two), 1.02 fps.
- + VICTORY_SHADER_LOOP_LIMIT_CS=1024: hair in place, 0.85 fps, and a
  smeared copy of the character along the bottom of the screen (another
  compute loop that now runs to its end).
Before that, on 2119b0e: with the eight SolvePerVertConstraint variants
skipped the scattered strands were gone (hair hung loose instead), and
with the general limit at 1024 the hair was in place.

